 ## Overview
 
-Piecewise Linear Interpolation (Lookup Table) is the standard method for evaluating 1D transfer functions in volume rendering, mapping scalar data values to visual parameters (color and opacity) by interpolating between user-defined control points [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of converting discrete user interactions into continuous visual mappings by constructing a continuous curve from a set of anchor points [S2, S7, unofficial]. Unlike [Cubic Spline Transfer Function](1811-cubic-spline-transfer-function.md), which ensures derivative continuity, this method prioritizes computational simplicity and direct control, making it the most disseminated approach for implementing specified transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Linear Look-up Table Classification](2636-linear-look-up-table-classification.md) by explicitly defining the interpolation logic between control points rather than relying solely on pre-binned discrete indices, and from [Smoothstep Interpolation](3287-smoothstep-interpolation.md) by lacking the smooth derivative at the boundaries of segments [S9].
+Piecewise Linear Interpolation (Lookup Table) is the standard method for evaluating 1D transfer functions in volume rendering, mapping scalar data values to visual parameters (color and opacity) by interpolating between user-defined control points [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of converting discrete user interactions into continuous visual mappings by constructing a continuous curve from a set of anchor points [S2, S7, unofficial]. It differs from [Cubic Spline Transfer Function](1811-cubic-spline-transfer-function.md) by lacking derivative continuity at segment boundaries, which can lead to visual artifacts like ringing in derived geometries [S9].
 
